Here's the formal test smell audit of your integration tests using the **testsmells.org 19-smell academic catalog**, with integration-test calibration applied (the class is marked `[TestCategory("Integration")]`).

---

## Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 3           | 3              |
| Medium   | 1           | 1              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **4**       | **4**          |

---

## Findings by Severity

### 🔴 HIGH — Sleepy Test

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` → `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 87)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000); // ← non-deterministic wait
```

**Why it matters (even for integration tests):** Adds 3 seconds of dead time per run. If the notification takes slightly longer on CI, this flakes. If it's faster, you're wasting time.

**Fix:**
```csharp
var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
await WaitUntilAsync(() => _repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id), cts.Token);
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
```
Use a polling/retry helper with a timeout instead of a fixed sleep.

---

### 🔴 HIGH — Conditional Test Logic

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` → `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (lines 101–108)

```csharp
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Why it matters:** One branch is never exercised per run — hidden testing gap. You can't tell from a green result which behavior was actually verified.

**Fix:** Split into two deterministic tests:
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenStandard_ReturnsName() { /* insert standard user, assert name */ }

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenPremium_HasDiscountRate() { /* insert premium user, assert discount */ }
```

---

### 🔴 HIGH — Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` → `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (lines 113–119)

```csharp
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
    // ← no assertion
}
```

**Why it matters:** This passes even if `Insert` silently drops every record. False confidence.

**Fix:**
```csharp
for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));

var users = _repository.ListAll();
Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count);
```

---

### 🟡 MEDIUM — Assertion Roulette

**File:** `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` → `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (lines 35–38)

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

Four assertions with no message parameters. When one fails in CI, the stack trace points to a line but the failure reason is opaque without source context.

**Fix:** Add message parameters:
```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should persist");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should persist");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Auto-generated Id should be positive");
```

---

## Smell-Free Patterns ✅

The remaining 4 tests are well-structured:

- **`UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord`** — clear arrange/act/assert, focused scope.
- **`DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase`** — single concern, explicit assertion.
- **`ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted`** — the `Assert.AreEqual(3, users.Count)` is self-documenting (3 items just added); **not** a Magic Number smell.
- **`InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity`** — properly awaited, clear assertion on expected count.

The use of an **in-memory SQLite** database is excellent — it avoids the Mystery Guest smell while still exercising real SQL.

---

## Prioritized Remediation Plan

| Priority | Smell | Fix Effort | Rationale |
|----------|-------|-----------|-----------|
| 1 | Assertion-Free Test | ~2 min | Prevents false-pass — highest risk |
| 2 | Conditional Test Logic | ~5 min | Eliminates hidden gap |
| 3 | Sleepy Test | ~10 min | Removes flakiness + saves 3s/run |
| 4 | Assertion Roulette | ~3 min | Improves CI debuggability |

**Bottom line:** 4 of 8 tests have catalog smells, but the structural foundation (in-memory DB, proper cleanup, focused tests) is solid. Fixing the three High-severity items would meaningfully improve reliability and debuggability.
